WEBVTT

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:08.930
aircraft.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.180
Submarines

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.860
all seeking to deliver scores of nuclear devices within a matter of hours.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:27.690
Thank you.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.560
The enemy were even partially successful.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.280
The blast and thermal damage in key centers of our country would be appalling.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.430
But the destruction would not stop there.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.340
People untouched by the blast.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.500
who had not even seen or heard it.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:08.790
would soon get sick.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:10.390
Why?

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.960
Radioactive debris from nuclear detonations near the ground.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.740
can drift with the wind.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.020
and then sprinkle down on our home.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:20.600
our factories.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.460
and our cities hundreds of miles away.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.000
Radioactivity can cause nausea.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.500
and radiation sickness among the unprotected.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.870
Where fallout is heaviest,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.160
It can even kill those who have not taken proper shelter.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.230
And what about our vital Air Force bases?

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.700
In an all-out attack.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.100
bases that had escaped direct damage

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.160
Might lie.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.050
right in the path of radioactive fallout.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.000
If they have no way of knowing this,

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.190
or were caught unprepared...

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.630
many Air Force bases would be knocked out.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.760
We might be without their protection.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:00.320
day.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.000
vital day.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.380
But today...

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.490
With our knowledge of radioactivity,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:15.000
military installations can continue their wartime missions even in the presence of fallout.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.210
With proper precautions,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.960
They can keep going.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.110
In spite of it.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.860
But just what makes fallout dangerous in the first place?

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.380
Let's look closely at a nuclear cloud.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.900
If we could see inside it,

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.700
we would find tons of earth and debris

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.920
being swept up into the fireball.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.700
In this intense heat,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.420
Solid matter becomes formless and may even vaporize.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.940
As the materials swirl,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.200
inside the fireball

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.630
They mix with radioactive fission products of the detonation.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.580
When the fireball cools,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.100
The surface debris and weapon materials condense into minute particles.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.830
trapping some of the radioactive fission products inside them.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.080
as the violent updrafts diminish.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.010
and the cloud stabilizes.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.050
the larger, heavier particles fall to work.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.830
Lighter particles, instead of falling straight down,

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.950
tend to drift with the wind.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.390
slowly settling over a large area.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.890
Normally the fallout pattern becomes

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.460
lighter and less concentrated,

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.090
as the cloud moves away from ground zero.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.240
Zones of higher intensities can occur, however.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:12.690
when rain or other weather conditions cause unusually heavy fallout.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.200
Ordinarily, only those particles that strike the ground within the first six hours or so

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.070
constitute a major hazard.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.350
Of the several kinds of radioactivity resulting from a nuclear detonation,

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:29.950
three are important in

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:30.400
fall out.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:32.250
alpha particles

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:34.640
beta particles,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:36.620
gamma rays.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.190
Radioactive debris.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:45.560
Emitting alpha particles is primarily limited to the immediate vicinity of the nuclear detonation.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.200
But beta particle...

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.780
and gamma-ray emitters,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:50.980
are to be found

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.910
wherever fallout takes place.

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:58.530
The alpha particles travel only a few inches through the air from their emitter.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:04:59.640
and cannot

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:07.670
They are dangerous only when they get inside the body, through a wound or the mouth or nose.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:15.160
Beta particles, however, travel several feet and can penetrate into the skin and cause severe burns.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.180
Ordinary clothing will shield the skin from these beta burns.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.500
By far the most serious are the gamma rays.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.970
They travel hundreds of feet through the air, and unlike the particles, these...

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.720
rays do penetrate deeply.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:33.750
and can cause physical damage.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.660
This is not something new, of course.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.150
Four cosmic rays from outer space are continually bombarding our bodies.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:46.550
and still more radiation may be received from natural mineral deposits.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.440
Normally, our bodies can withstand these small doses without evident ill effect.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:54.280
But...

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.050
A large-scale increase in radiation can prove harmful.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.900
and everyone who can

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:01.250
must take shelter.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.180
In a wartime situation, of course.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:09.650
Some men have to work out of doors if an installation is to continue to function.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:14.300
Even so, with careful planning and close control of work crews,

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.680
A commander can hold to a minimum

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.300
the amount of radiation these men received.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.200
by keeping work shifts to a controlled minimum.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.060
An airbase can continue to operate

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.680
at a time when the nation needs it the most.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.180
Let's visit a typical Air Force base.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.480
and see how it met the fallout problem.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:46.280
Air Force Base.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.240
is the Joint Strategic Air Command, Air Defense Command installation.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:56.000
Its primary mission is to deliver a knockout punch if enemy aircraft threaten our country.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:04.860
In the event of war,

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.010
The prime consideration...

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.980
would be to get all operational aircraft airborne.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:12.540
Nothing.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:13.780
must prevent that.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.080
Thank you.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:21.690
One man had the responsibility.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.970
foreseeing that nothing did prevent it.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.020
Major Russell Whitaker.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.840
the base disaster control operations officer.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.640
It was his job to plan and implement a program of operation under emergency conditions.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.350
including radioactive fallout.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:44.520
Recent expansions at Echo Air Force Base called for new measures to improve defenses against fallout hazards.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:49.870
The major's program was based on cooperation from all elements.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:50.920
Both sacked.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:51.880
and ADC.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.900
and involved every unit and person at Echo.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.400
The Major had decided

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.860
that the radiological monitoring teams

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.660
needed an intensified training program and the base would have to provide additional shelters

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:12.550
to accommodate the new people at Echo. The disaster control plan was solid, well-coordinated.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:19.190
It took into consideration the locations of water and power cutoffs, fire hydrants,

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:25.160
details of plumbing and wiring in various buildings, the emergency positioning of base

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.360
motor pool vehicle, everything that could be of importance in an emergency.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.140
Consideration had to be given to the possible evacuation of the hospital.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:38.510
Under plans, carefully worked out with the base surgeon and the provost marshal.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:45.830
A forthcoming base construction project would make it necessary to designate new emergency traffic routes.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.000
And recent intelligence reports called for changes in the internal security measures.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:59.980
Major Whitaker also made sure the unit disaster control officers from organizations...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.760
throughout the base, knew how to select a good fallout shelter,

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.800
big enough to take care of a large number of people,

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.730
not too much window space to let radiation through, but those walls.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.200
How effective would they be against gamma rays?

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.570
The book says concrete block walls have a moderate absorption effect on gamma.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:29.830
Solid concrete would be better, but such buildings are scarce at echo.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:40.010
another important consideration the building's dimensions from the center to the nearest outside

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:47.710
wall and from the ceiling to a point three feet above the floor gamma rays from fallout around the

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:52.950
shelter and from radioactivity on the roof would have to travel through considerable airspace to

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.340
reach the center of this building.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.390
Standard tables for concrete block wall construction

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:05.360
and for airspace distances make it possible to determine the shielding effect of a building

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:12.650
without taking actual radiation measurements the residual number indicates the percentage

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:20.720
of the outside radioactivity that would reach the inside no other structure in that part of

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:26.160
the base offered as much protection and this one had the advantage of serving a large work area

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.960
Anyone in the shelter who must go out into the fallout...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.180
work for a limited time would be nearest job. The shelter had adequate plumbing facilities

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:42.150
and a central heating plant. All it lacked were supplies, water and food to cover the

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:48.860
foreseeable emergency period, first aid kits, emergency communication equipment, exposure

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:55.190
control instructions for people in the shelter. With these items and a sign clearly designating

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:59.770
the structure as a fallout shelter of a specified capacity and residual number,

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.880
the building became a part of the ECHO Air Force Base Disaster Control Program.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:14.650
Major Whitaker's plans also called for extensive preparation by the installation's engineer.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.610
If the base should suffer heavy fallout,

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:21.890
earth-moving equipment might be needed for turning under the contaminated soil

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.440
and for burying radioactive wastes.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:29.740
Heavy equipment like this can be used effectively.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:33.790
as emergency vehicles for clearing debris from runways and other areas.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.380
Also playing an important part in the base disaster control plan

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.160
were the vehicles equipped with two-way radios.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.040
Communications independent of normal channels could be of utmost importance in a disaster.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:56.100
Another important activity on the base from the disaster control standpoint

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.010
was the base weather station.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:11:59.970
If there were...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.880
nuclear attack anywhere near echo one of the first questions would be will fallout be blown

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:14.790
this way the first clue would come from weather information to get that clue the forecaster uses

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:20.030
conventional winds aloft flight data provided by the weather bureau and the air weather service

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:27.710
from them he arrives at a six hour forecast of where fallout may take place under the current

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:28.450
weather situation.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:36.980
Assuming a nuclear strike here, the winds at various altitudes could deposit fallout in a pattern like this.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:44.090
With the present wind structure, Echo Air Force Base would be in the fringe of the six-hour fallout pattern.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:51.690
Heaviest concentration of radioactivity would be in the middle portion.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:57.840
The forecaster makes no attempt to predict just how intense the radioactivity might be.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.310
Astrologists make these plots regularly on a routine basis and during an emergency as often as the situation requires.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.260
However, final determination must rest upon instrument readings at the proper time.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:17.570
Another outpost for defense against fallout was the radiological monitoring instrument.

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:22.470
It kept a 24-hour-a-day check on gamma intensities in the area.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:27.270
Such a unit is fully automatic and requires only routine maintenance.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:29.970
Gamma rays that enter the detection...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.100
detector are counted by the device, and the information is fed to a remote indicator that

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:41.540
registers the hourly rate of gamma emissions. An intensity reading of one-half rentgen per

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:47.530
hour sets off an alarm, alerting the base to the arrival of fallout. The unit can be

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:54.520
run by battery, and thus may be independent of other power sources. This was where the

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:59.980
commander and his staff were to run the operation in an emergency. Here in the command post

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.480
they could keep in touch with the entire situation.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.960
All elements on the base concerned with the primary mission

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.710
had direct communications with the command post.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.620
One of the activities that was kept current here

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:17.280
was the latest weather forecast on where fallout might occur

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:18.740
under the present weather condition.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.500
The command post also had an area map

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.120
on which radiation intensities could be plotted

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:26.560
during a fallout emergency.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:29.960
Certain parts of the base were designated...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.680
as vital areas to be kept operational at all times.

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:38.560
Such an area might have to be hosed off during an actual fallout attack

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:41.410
so work could continue around important equipment.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:47.120
The major gave close attention to the training of decontamination teams.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:53.890
In an actual emergency, these men, cross-trained for the purpose,

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.280
would be needed for operations like this

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.080
to take the place of regular firefighting personnel

00:14:59.080 --> 00:14:59.960
who would have other...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:11.220
defense tasks. All radioactive waste would have to be flushed into proper drainage system or dumped

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:18.380
in some non-vital area where they could cool off. Echo Air Force Base also had an active training

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:29.970
program for its radiological recovery team. Man was trained in the methods of...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.170
reclaiming vital areas and in the use of radiation detection instruments.

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:40.000
He also wore a personal dosimeter, which would record radiation received.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:44.050
Periodically, each dosimeter would be checked for training purposes.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:51.130
All in all, Major Russell Whitaker, Disaster Control Operations Officer at Echo Air Force Base,

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.830
knew he had a sound, workable disaster control program.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:08.120
And then military intelligence reported our nation faced the possibility of imminent attack.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:14.140
All persons not essential to the operation of the base were told to take action according to pre-arranged plan.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:17.100
Families were advised to stay near shelter.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:35.380
The commander at his staff moved into the command post to be ready for instantaneous action.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:37.070
If there were to be war,

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:38.780
Echo would be ready.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:43.420
But as yet,

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:44.900
There was no definite picture.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:50.050
So the commander directed that, for the moment at least, all necessary work would continue.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.880
Supervisors of activities throughout the installation were advised by telephone of the situation

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.010
and instructed to be prepared to take further emergency war plan steps on a moment's notice.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:28.270
to the base pyramid alert system.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:29.900
Off duty flight and.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.680
ground support crews began to arrive.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.350
Over at the base disaster control point.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.960
Radiation monitoring teams were getting their equipment.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:44.210
Each team.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.920
Be responsible for one part of the base if a fallout emergency arose.

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:54.030
Augmentation crews were arriving at Air Police Headquarters, too.

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:56.820
to defend the base against possible sabotage.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.380
Echo Air Force Base was playing for keeps.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:29.260
If you have been in a space, proceed immediately.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.100
are designated shelter. Echo Air Force Base is at war. Attention.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:35.450
Thank you.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:44.140
These utilities have to be shut off for the duration of the threat to the base.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.060
Meanwhile, miles away.

00:19:58.090 --> 00:19:59.930
nuclear attacks pour into the echo

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:17.140
command post. Will fall out from any of the explosions be blown this way unless conditions

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:23.770
change. Echo Air Force Base will be right in the path. Our intelligence information indicates the

00:20:23.770 --> 00:20:29.980
nuclear yield of the weapon was quite high. The problem, to continue full operations and

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.260
the fallout commences here and then to do everything possible to carry on the

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:42.340
mission right through the fallout some men will have to remain outside even

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:49.810
after the fallout starts it's the commander's responsibility to fix the

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:53.790
maximum permissible dose his men may be allowed to take in the light of the

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:59.870
necessities of his mission the base has a little time before

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.230
of fallout can be expected here. All activities that can do so must move inside undercover.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:15.590
After fallout arrives, those men whose work will keep them out of doors must be put on

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:23.970
a tight schedule to hold their exposure to a minimum. You can't feel it, you can't smell

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:28.710
it, you may not be able to see it, but it's there, coming at you.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:58.880
There's no time to lose the turnaround time must be kept to a minimum pilot must get into a shelter immediately

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:11.370
Only those who absolutely have to stay outside can be permitted to remain in the fallout.

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:20.700
Over on the bomber flight line, problems of a special sort confront these men.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:29.340
This B-47, one of the generation aircraft not in hot alert status, has just been fueled with a full combat load.

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:34.170
But more work still has to be done before this aircraft can take off on its strike mission.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:38.000
Dozens of electronic black boxes have to be checked out,

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.200
and those that are bad must be replaced.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:53.200
These tasks that require patience, they can't be hurried.

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:09.490
every operating unit on the aircraft must have electric power whenever there is need for extra

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:14.910
parts or the services of a specialist radio dispatch trucks provide the fastest means of

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:25.360
getting the work done maintenance shop on wheels not only expedites the overall program but it

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:27.720
also gives the men some protection from the fallout.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:29.900
From time to time,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.320
radiological survey teams make radiation level readings as a check on the central monitoring

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:41.720
equipment. Back in the maintenance control section, the unit disaster control officer

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:47.590
is in close touch with the situation. Each work crew's exposure time in the fallout field is

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:53.810
predicted on the specialist planning chart. Any report of change in radiation intensities

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:58.150
has a direct bearing on how long a crew can be permitted to remain outside.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.400
Radiation level readings may come from both the command post and from special radiological survey teams.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:19.130
As the crews in the fallout area approach their exposure limit, new crews must be dispatched to relieve them.

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:25.290
In this way, a base can be assured of continued operation in spite of the fallout,

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:29.360
without unduly risking excessive exposure among vital personnel.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:40.230
A nearby radar station has experienced fallout, too.

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:43.410
One of the vital links in our national defense,

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:46.010
this station must be kept operational.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.000
Flushing away radioactive dust from the roof

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:52.890
removes one of the threats to the men working inside

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:56.050
and permits the radar station to continue operations.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:59.980
Inside, during the emergency, work...

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.220
goes on around the clock.

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:05.200
Some men seated at positions near the outside walls

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:06.800
may require special protection

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.910
because the walls alone may not be adequate shielding

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:11.130
against a heavy fallout.

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:14.550
Each 7 1⁄2 inches of sand

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:17.250
cuts gamma radioactivity by 1⁄2.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:22.160
Back at Echo, as the hours wear on,

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:24.570
the disaster control operations officer

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.850
keeps a careful check on the radiation level.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:29.970
Each time he gets a reading, he advises...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.560
is the forecaster who passes the information on to other weather facilities. In turn, intensity

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:41.860
readings from elsewhere are plotted as they are received. This information gives a good

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:54.320
indication how the fallout cloud is moving. Latest plots of the echo readings show intensities

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:59.920
are on a steady decline. If all goes well and no other fallout clouds come

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:17.120
the picture, the crisis may be passed. Once the pattern reveals a plunge in

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:21.790
intensities, a pretty fair prediction can be made that they will fall for a

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:27.670
while and then level off in a long slow decay of the radioactive emitters.

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:38.390
When a level is reached at which it would be safe to make a base-wide survey,

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:42.490
helicopters can take preliminary radiation intensity reading.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:52.040
Helicopters are of particular value in surveying remote sectors that otherwise could be approached only through heavily contaminated areas.

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:59.980
Intensity reports thus obtained are relayed by radio to the command post, where a detailed picture...

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:07.360
the entire area is constructed where it is safe to do so fast-moving ground monitoring teams get

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:17.400
more detailed information meanwhile decontamination crews help return the base to full operation by

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:22.350
rendering safe those areas where work must be resumed at the earliest possible moment

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:29.930
Since this is a slow process at best, some areas that may be hot for a while

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:33.520
but are not needed right away are simply marked off limits.

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:38.400
While this work is going on, in each fallout shelter,

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.900
the senior officer present watches over his people.

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:49.100
He makes regular reports over the special disaster control communications network

00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:51.700
as to the welfare of the personnel in the shelter.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.840
Anyone who has been in high-intensity areas

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:59.720
or whose clothing carries radioactive dust must be processed.

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:02.260
through the Personnel Decontamination Center.

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:07.020
prompt attention to those who were thought to have radioactive matter on their bodies.

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:10.680
will prevent them from getting painful beta contact burns.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.950
Simplest and best way to get rid of the dust?

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:15.970
Take a thorough shower.

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:19.240
The hare should come in for special attention.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:21.720
because it's an excellent dust collector.

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:27.100
The feet can carry contamination, too, from dust kicked up in the fallout area.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.070
Anyone who hasn't done a good job

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:35.880
get sent back to the showers.

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:40.500
After that, each man is provided clean clothes.

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:45.880
His body may have sustained a mild dose of radioactivity in line of duty.

00:28:46.660 --> 00:28:48.300
But bodily recovery is rapid.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:51.930
And in most cases, he will not even notice any ill effects.

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:05.300
Echo Air Force Base.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:06.340
Survive.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:08.440
and did its job.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.420
because its people were prepared.

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:16.840
What we have seen here has never actually happened in our country.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:18.520
At least, not yet.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:20.710
And it is our fervent hope

00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:22.030
that it never will.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:24.110
But it could happen.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:25.560
We must...

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:26.610
Be prepared.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:29.220
And if we are prepared,

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.580
if our bases are ready.

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:33.940
if our people know what to do.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:35.940
We will keep going.

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:37.290
Keep fighting.

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:39.880
even in radioactive fallout.
